import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const buildCode = `let options = McpServerApplicationOptions::new(
    "inventory-mcp",
    "1.0.0",
    100,
    Duration::from_secs(60),
    McpServerCacheScope::Private,
)?;

let app = McpServerApplicationBuilder::new(options)
    .with_tools(tool_registry, tool_authorization)?
    .with_resources(resource_catalog, resource_reader, resource_authorization)?
    .with_prompts(prompt_catalog, prompt_renderer, prompt_authorization)?
    .with_completion_provider(completion_provider)?
    .build()?;

let service = McpServerHttpService::with_admission_control(
    app,
    http_options,
    McpServerAuthentication::bearer(authenticator, bearer_challenge),
    admission_control,
)?;`;

const verifyCode = `cargo test -p stateknot-integrations mcp_server_ --locked
bash conformance/mcp-server/run-2026-07-28.sh`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Expose a bounded MCP Server without weakening policy."
  description="Compose StateKnot-owned Tools, Resources, Prompts, Completion, and MRTR behind strict stateless HTTP, decoded authorization, and exact evidence."
  section="MCP Server profile"
>
  <aside
    class="docs-callout docs-callout--boundary"
    aria-labelledby="mcp-server-boundary-title"
  >
    <h2 id="mcp-server-boundary-title">
      Implemented Server profile, pre-alpha API
    </h2>
    <p>
      The StateKnot application layer and production transport implement the MCP
      <code>2026-07-28</code> core Server profile for Tools, Resources, Resource Templates,
      Prompts, Completion, and MRTR. Tasks, a bundled authorization server, extensions,
      and a stable Rust API are not claimed.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <section aria-labelledby="mcp-server-flow-title">
    <h2 id="mcp-server-flow-title">Keep policy in the request path</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Validate Host, Origin, version metadata, and the streaming body limit.
      </li>
      <li>Authenticate the Bearer credential into a bounded principal.</li>
      <li>Apply caller-owned cross-replica admission and quota policy.</li>
      <li>Decode the JSON-RPC operation; transport headers remain hints.</li>
      <li>Authorize before existence or schema diagnostics are disclosed.</li>
      <li>
        Validate input, execute with cancellation, then validate the result.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Anonymous service construction fails unless every configured Host is
      literal loopback. Public deployments require TLS at the edge, Bearer
      authentication, explicit Host/Origin allowlists, and a shared admission
      implementation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="mcp-server-surface-title">
    <h2 id="mcp-server-surface-title">Advertise only configured surfaces</h2>
    <div
      class="docs-table-wrap"
      tabindex="0"
      aria-label="MCP Server application surface guarantees"
    >
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Surface</th><th>StateKnot boundary</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Tools</td>
            <td>
              Immutable executable registry, offline JSON Schema 2020-12 input
              and output validation, progress, cancellation, and bounded
              content.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Resources</td>
            <td>
              Absolute URI and template validation, authorization-first reads,
              MIME/Base64 limits, and explicit result cache policy.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Prompts</td>
            <td>
              Unique bounded arguments, authorization before diagnostics, and
              bounded multi-content messages.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Completion</td>
            <td>
              Optional capability; authenticated references and at most 100
              unique, pagination-consistent values.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>MRTR</td>
            <td>
              Bounded Elicitation/Sampling/Roots requests and
              integrity-protected, expiring, operation-bound request state.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      An unconfigured surface returns method-not-found. The builder does not
      publish empty placeholder capabilities, and the official Rust SDK remains
      a private wire adapter rather than StateKnot's domain model.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="mcp-server-catalog-title">
    <h2 id="mcp-server-catalog-title">Freeze catalogs before traffic</h2>
    <p>
      Tool schemas compile offline at startup; unresolved or network <code
        >$ref</code
      >
      values fail the build. Tool, Resource, Template, and Prompt catalogs enforce
      count and byte ceilings, reject duplicates, sort deterministically, and expose
      stable digests. Scope-filtered discovery uses principal-bound cursors. Public
      caching is rejected when any entry is scope-restricted.
    </p>
    <p>
      Descriptions, annotations, schemas, server instructions, client
      capabilities, and completion values remain untrusted data. They never
      grant authority.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="mcp-server-build-title">
    <h2 id="mcp-server-build-title">Compose one application</h2>
    <div class="docs-code" data-code-block>
      <div class="docs-code-head">
        <span>Rust · construction outline</span>
        <button
          class="copy-button affordance"
          type="button"
          data-copy-button
          data-copy-target="mcp-server-build-code"
        >
          <span data-copy-label aria-live="polite">Copy</span>
        </button>
      </div>
      <pre
        id="mcp-server-build-code"
        tabindex="0"><code>{buildCode}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
      Mount the resulting Tower service at one exact Axum or Hyper endpoint.
      Build registries once at startup; never rebuild them per request.
      Application authorization must use decoded operations, not <code
        >Mcp-Method</code
      > or
      <code>Mcp-Name</code> transport hints.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="mcp-server-proof-title">
    <h2 id="mcp-server-proof-title">Reproduce the evidence</h2>
    <div class="docs-code" data-code-block>
      <div class="docs-code-head">
        <span>Shell · application and official gates</span>
        <button
          class="copy-button affordance"
          type="button"
          data-copy-button
          data-copy-target="mcp-server-verify-code"
        >
          <span data-copy-label aria-live="polite">Copy</span>
        </button>
      </div>
      <pre
        id="mcp-server-verify-code"
        tabindex="0"><code>{verifyCode}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
      The pinned runner passes all 37 scored Server scenarios: 114 assertions
      succeed, five capability checks skip, one SSE check is informational, and
      zero fail or warn. Three pending unscored schema/header gates add 32
      successes. The production transport is exercised by the official fixture;
      StateKnot registries and policies have separate real-HTTP tests. Read the <a
        href="/docs/mcp-conformance/">exact claim boundary</a
      > before publishing compatibility claims.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
